Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

Dear TLS Working Group,

Following the feedback on our presentation of Supplemental Authentication
in TLS 1.3, Tiru, Rifaat, Hannes, and I have published a simpler proposal
focused on secondary client authentication:
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-rosomakho-tls-secondary-client-auth/

The draft defines a TLS flag to negotiate one additional client
authentication exchange during connection establishment. The server sends a
second CertificateRequest immediately after its Finished message,
potentially in the same flight. The client responds using post-handshake
authentication after its own handshake Finished message and before sending
application data.

The main use case is enterprise VPNs that authenticate both a user and a
managed device using separate certificates. The draft enables this exchange
with HTTP/2 and QUIC by creating a limited exception to their restrictions
on post-handshake authentication. Because the exchange is negotiated during
connection establishment, it does not require correlating a certificate
request with activity on a particular application stream.

As always, constructive feedback, suggestions and questions are very
welcome!


Thank you.

Best regards,
Yaroslav


